body:
I've been revising this whole Max FPS implementation with Claude & Grok..

Apparently "preferredFramesPerSecond" is a deprecated API since iOS 15...
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/quartzcore/optimizing-iphone-and-ipad-apps-to-support-promotion-displays#Provide-time-paced-content-to-a-ProMotion-display

The modern replacement is "preferredFrameRateRange" using CAFrameRateRange, 
which gives the system an efficient rate within a range to choose from. 

It's possible that using the deprecated property on modern iOS/Metal interacted poorly with the Metal frame pacing system,
contributing to the FPS cap processing overhead..

-So we have implemented this little fix which seems to be working allright on my end,
running my 2D Audio app (with 2x Shaders) on my iPhone 12 + iOS 26 with Metal renderer, and a setting of Max FPS = 30 

The idea is basically to apply this limits:
Min = Target / 2 (with Hard Limit of 15FPS)
Target = Target
Range = Target x 2
Max Value or 0 = 120 FPS

When the user sets Max FPS = 30 in Project Settings, the Target will be 30;
but we leave some Range for iOS to process any Peaks or overhead, 
making the "Target x2" rule apply, and letting iOS go up to 60fps.. (or down to 15fps) if necessary.

Max Value user can input - 120 fps

So the Target x2 rule will apply until it reaches 120fps, 
which seems to be the top supported limit.. (120HZ for ProMotion devices)

-The Fix:

Step 1: (Optional)
Apply this PR - https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/117820/changes/e3d3689a0c72d4616c968717774ebbfcf52015f2

Edit the file drivers/apple_embedded/display_server_apple_embedded.h

add "core/os/thread_safe.h" at the TOP

Step 2:

Edit drivers/apple_embedded/display_server_apple_embedded.mm, and add "memdelete(rendering_device);" on line 144.

```
rendering_device = memnew(RenderingDevice);
		if (rendering_device->initialize(rendering_context, MAIN_WINDOW_ID) != OK) {
			memdelete(rendering_device); // ADD THIS!
			rendering_device = nullptr;
			memdelete(rendering_context);
			rendering_context = nullptr;
			r_error = ERR_UNAVAILABLE;
			return;
		}
```

-Step 3:

Edit drivers/apple_embedded/godot_view_apple_embedded.mm file,
change startRendering function from this:

```
- (void)startRendering {
	if (self.isActive) {
		return;
	}

	self.isActive = YES;

	print_verbose("Start animation!");

	if (self.useCADisplayLink) {
		self.displayLink = [CADisplayLink displayLinkWithTarget:self selector:@selector(drawView)];
		self.displayLink.preferredFramesPerSecond = self.preferredFrameRate;

		// Setup DisplayLink in main thread
		[self.displayLink addToRunLoop:[NSRunLoop currentRunLoop] forMode:NSRunLoopCommonModes];
	} else {
		self.animationTimer = [NSTimer scheduledTimerWithTimeInterval:(1.0 / self.preferredFrameRate) target:self selector:@selector(drawView) userInfo:nil repeats:YES];
	}
}
```

To this updated version:

```
- (void)startRendering {
    if (self.isActive) {
        return;
    }

    self.isActive = YES;

    print_verbose("Start animation!");

    if (self.useCADisplayLink) {
        self.displayLink = [CADisplayLink displayLinkWithTarget:self selector:@selector(drawView)];

        if (@available(iOS 15.0, *)) {
            float target = self.preferredFrameRate;

            CAFrameRateRange range;

            if (target <= 0) {
                // Uncapped (Max FPS = 0 in Godot) ? let the device run at its natural maximum
                range = CAFrameRateRangeDefault;
            } else {
                // Capped at user-selected Max FPS from Godot
                float minimum = fmaxf(15.0f, target * 0.5f);           // e.g. 15 when target=30
                float maximum = fminf(target * 2.0f, 120.0f);          // e.g. 60 when target=30, 120 when target=60+

                range = CAFrameRateRangeMake(minimum, maximum, target);
            }

            self.displayLink.preferredFrameRateRange = range;
    } else {
        // Fallback for iOS < 15
#pragma clang diagnostic push
#pragma clang diagnostic ignored "-Wdeprecated-declarations"
        self.displayLink.preferredFramesPerSecond = self.preferredFrameRate;
#pragma clang diagnostic pop
    }

		// Setup DisplayLink in main thread
		[self.displayLink addToRunLoop:[NSRunLoop currentRunLoop] forMode:NSRunLoopCommonModes];
	} else {
		self.animationTimer = [NSTimer scheduledTimerWithTimeInterval:(1.0 / self.preferredFrameRate) target:self selector:@selector(drawView) userInfo:nil repeats:YES];
	}
}
```

-After that I rebuilt the new Editor and Templates, and that's it,
seems to work Perfectly on this iPhone 12 with iOS 26.3, or 26.4.2 which I just updated to.. 

-As far as Performance gains go.. it's hard to asses, as my app is not very graphically demanding,
but framerate seems to be a bit more consistent, and perhaps fluid; 
at least on the animated Panel based menus I'm using..

-Could you re-run the test and see if there's any improvement on your end?

